 𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟱𝟯/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴, 𝗯𝘂𝘁 𝗖𝗣𝗨 𝗶𝘀 𝗛𝗶𝗴𝗵 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

Your Kubernetes application Pod is Running and Ready, but CPU utilization suddenly increases to 95–100%. Application response time is also increasing. How would you investigate this in production?

Here’s my practical approach 👇

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗜𝗱𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗛𝗶𝗴𝗵 𝗖𝗣𝗨 𝗣𝗼𝗱

kubectl top pods -n <namespace> --sort-by=cpu

Identify which Pods are consuming the most CPU.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗮𝗶𝗻𝗲𝗿-𝗟𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗹 𝗨𝘀𝗮𝗴𝗲

kubectl top pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> --containers

Identify the container responsible for high CPU consumption, especially in multi-container Pods.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗥𝗲𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗣𝗨 𝗥𝗲𝗾𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝘀 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗟𝗶𝗺𝗶𝘁𝘀

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Check CPU requests and limits.

Also investigate CPU throttling through container metrics and monitoring dashboards.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗟𝗼𝗴𝘀

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --since=30m

Look for:

- Infinite loops or excessive retries
- Increased request volume
- Background jobs consuming CPU
- Inefficient application processing
- Repeated errors

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗔𝗻𝗱 𝗡𝗼𝗱𝗲 𝗠𝗲𝘁𝗿𝗶𝗰𝘀

kubectl top nodes
kubectl get pods -n <namespace> -o wide

Determine whether the issue is isolated to one Pod or affects multiple workloads on the same node.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲 𝗖𝗼𝗿𝗿𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝘃𝗲 𝗔𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻

Depending on the root cause:

- Fix inefficient application code.
- Investigate abnormal traffic.
- Tune CPU requests and limits.
- Scale replicas if additional capacity is needed.
- Configure HPA when scaling based on CPU utilization is appropriate.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A Pod can be Running and Ready while consuming excessive CPU.

Don't just increase CPU limits. Identify why CPU consumption increased first.
